# A filesystem-backed queue store root, written by a local run rather than by
# a build: `queue/storage.rs` creates `system/storage-layout.json` beside a
# `.metadata` file and a `.locks/` directory under whatever root the run is
# pointed at, so it lands wherever the command was launched from -- commonly
# `stado-rs/.proof-store/`. It is regenerated state describing one machine's
# objects and locks, never source, and it belongs to no other checkout. Left
# untracked it would otherwise be swept into history by the first `git add`
# of everything, which in this repository happens often.
.proof-store/
# The intermediate files a conditional registry write forces an operator to
# materialize. `stado registry push --if-generation` takes the document as a
# file and the fence token as a separate argument, and `registry pull
# --with-generation` prints both wrapped in one envelope, so applying one key
# to the canonical registry means writing out the pull, the unwrapped
# document, the edited document and the token -- four files, all of them a
# verbatim copy of the fleet's own configuration, and none of them source.
# Ignored rather than kept: they name every host, its ssh destination and its
# declared services, and the first `git add` of everything would put that in
# the history of a repository that has no business holding it. Regenerate
# them with `stado registry pull` whenever they are wanted again.
.registry-snapshot.json
.registry-with-gen.json
.registry-next.json
.registry-generation
migration/
.stado-precheck-release/
.wisent-output/
